ments-removed","current_statement_sha256":"sha256:4fcbd7e07bdcbf3354875a025bedaa9055edf0cff3d202a4ceac469face79f89","file":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","line":96,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"bae10a13c495357a641ed17c0a3885b1ccc3a3a56349e497d6b4ed597f237262","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:4fcbd7e07bdcbf3354875a025bedaa9055edf0cff3d202a4ceac469face79f89","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex#L96"},"statement_kind":"corollary","text":"\\label{cor:k-sensitive}\nThere are absolute constants $\\varepsilon_0>0$ and $C_0<\\infty$ such\nthat, for every integer $n\\ge2$ and every integer $1\\le k\\le n/2$,\nthe number of $k$-sets of any $n$-point set $Q\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ with\nno three collinear is at most\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:k-sensitive}\n C_0 n(k+1)^{1/3-\\varepsilon_0}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe same bound holds for $\\operatorname{comp}(\\Lambda_k(\\mathcal L))$\nfor any arrangement $\\mathcal L$ of $n$ nonvertical straight lines\nwith no parallel pair and no three concurrent."}
